  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Saturday)\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA lull in activity is expected on Sunday as weak mid level ridging\nbuilds in between mid level systems. Partly cloudy skies will \nallow for temperatures to warm more Sunday afternoon, back into \nthe mid to upper 70s for most locations. \n\nFocus will then turn to the next storm system slated to affect the\narea later in the day Monday through Monday night into Tuesday. A\nrobust upper low is currently moving onshore over the Pacific\nNorthwest and will work its way south and east into the Plains by\nMonday afternoon. This system will link up with a plume of\ntropical moisture from the tropical eastern Pacific, drawing ample\nmoisture northward over the Plains during the day Monday and\nMonday night. A surface low is progged to develop and deepen\nacross the High Plains of eastern Colorado Monday morning and \ntrack northeastward into the Northern Plains by Monday night. A \ncold front will extend southward from this surface low across much\nof the Southern Plains and track eastward into Monday night. The\ntightening pressure gradient will lead to strong winds and wind\ngusts during the day Monday, with gusts near 40mph possible across\nnortheast Oklahoma Monday afternoon. Some shower or elevated\nthunderstorm activity will be possible during the day Monday,\nmainly across northeast Oklahoma, associated with a strong low\nlevel jet preceding the main trough, but chances appear sporadic\nat best right now. recent model data and trends suggest a slow \ndown of the upper trough and associated cold front as both hang \nback across western Oklahoma into Monday night. Thus storm chances\nhave slowed down some, with chances beginning to increase Monday \nevening across northeast Oklahoma and tracking eastward during the\novernight hours. Strong deep layer shear would be supportive of \nat least a limited severe threat, though questions remain on how \nmuch instability can develop ahead of the front, especially after \nsunset. Damaging wind gusts continue to be the primary threat, \nthough all modes will be in play with ample low level shear in \nplace to support a tornado threat, primarily across eastern \nOklahoma.\n\nHeavy rain will also be a threat with tropical moisture in place\nand localized areas of flash flooding will be possible in more\nflood prone locations. The trough axis and cold front should clear\nthe region by Tuesday afternoon, ushering in a drier and cooler\nairmass through much of the week. Temperatures will trend down\ncloser to normal ranges with cooler morning and pleasant afternoon\nhighs. Another quick moving wave moves across the area on\nThursday, but limited moisture will limit the rain chances with\nthis system. Another, stronger system could be on tap for next\nweekend with more rain and storm chances, but significant model\ndifferences exists with the timing and evolution of this system\ncurrently.\n\nBowlan",
